  "notes": "The producer/engineer/musician who co-produced a self-produced pivot record and \"moved her from perfectionism to performance.\" The band method is the transferable parameter and the entry says so outright: \"demo playback → the band jams on it → the musicians add the arrangement → the singer sings live with the band in the room, tracked over a two-week session … with 'innovative overhead mic techniques' for later mix flexibility. So: overhead-forward roomy kit, arrangement generated by players not charts, live lead vocal.\" An arrangement generated by players rather than charts is a high `switchUpProb` and a fill grid that fires where the band decides; an overhead-forward kit is a cymbal-and-tom-weighted perc set. Keys are documented: \"major and minor in near-equal measure on that record (F♯ major, F minor, B♭ minor all present); vocabulary I–V–vi–IV and i–VII–VI with pop turnarounds.\" The vocal chain exists to handle a documented dynamic problem — a voice that \"can literally go from singing very softly to blowing the microphone out\" — which is the widest `velocityVar` of any producer here and an accent tier at the ceiling. The one-take guitar solo kept on the title track is the counter's high answer density: it is a played part, not a comped one. ⚠ The bass is \"live bass … kick-locked 8ths under choruses,\" so no `drums.bass808` block. ⚠ Mixing is delegated to pop mixers rather than a Nashville mixer, which the entry calls \"a deliberate crossover signal\" — noted, but it reaches no generator and nothing here is authored from it. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the fill grid and the counter ratio.",
